    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, so I'm working on a digital collection of some old 19th-century maps for our university library. I'm trying to use Dublin Core for the metadata, but I've run into a bit of a snag with the 'creator' field. We have a bunch of maps where the engraver or cartographer isn't actually listed or is totally unidentifiable. Should I just leave it blank, or is there a standard way to denote 'unknown' without messing up the archival integrity or the searchability of the collection?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Leave the `creator` field blank rather than filling it with \"unknown,\" as Dublin Core expects specific agents or organizations and does not define an \"unknown creator\" concept. To maintain searchability while preserving integrity, consider using a separate metadata element like ` institutional identifier` or a custom field to house the note that the creator is unidentifiable, or simply omitting the field entirely if your library's system allows missing optional elements. This approach adheres to the metadata schema while clearly signaling that the original creator cannot be identified in the cataloging record.",
      "source": "target",
